{
 "id": "shenandoah",
 "version": 8,
 "reviewed": true,
 "title": "Shenandoah",
 "description": "The American river song, for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano sings the long, rolling melody, the alto and tenor move gently beneath it, and the bass holds the roots on \"oo\". Slow and spacious, with time to breathe.",
 "language": "en",
 "difficulty": "intermediate",
 "genre": [
  "folk",
  "sea shanty"
 ],
 "style": "free",
 "tempo": 63,
 "key": "F",
 "timeSignature": "4/4",
 "pickupBeats": 1,
 "credits": {
  "words": "traditional American (19th century)",
  "tune": "traditional, as printed by Richard Runciman Terry in The Shanty Book, Part I (1921)",
  "arrangement": "Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: traditional. \"Oh Shenandoah, I long to hear you\" (twice) and \"Away, you rolling river\" are verse 1 of Terry's The Shanty Book, Part I (Curwen, 1921), No. 9, whose last line reads \"Away, I'm bound to go 'Cross the wide Missouri\"; \"I'm bound away 'cross the wide Missouri\" is printed in W. B. Whall, Ships, Sea Songs and Shanties (3rd ed., 1913), p. 1 (after \"Ah-ha\"), and \"Shanadore I long to hear you\" in \"Sailors' Songs\", The New Dominion Monthly (Montreal, April 1876), p. 262",
  "tune": "public domain: traditional, printed 1921 by Richard Runciman Terry (died 1938)",
  "arrangement": "original, written for Stackapella. Accepted exceptions (singable.py): the tune's own range as Terry printed it (in E-flat, written here a tone higher in F), its low C on \"Oh\" a tone under the beginner soprano range and its top F on \"A-\" a semitone over it; the alto's fifth from A on \"you,\" up to E on \"A-\" comes across the breath at the start of a new line, and from there it rises by step to F on \"way,\", a third under the tune's G and A; and the bass's two leaps over a fourth in a line are its roots on \"oo\", each a fifth: F down to B-flat under \"a-\" and back up under \"way,\" in \"I'm bound a-way,\", then D down to G under \"Mis-\", up to C under \"sou-\" and down to F under \"ri.\", the cadence's roots.",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "melody from Richard Runciman Terry, The Shanty Book, Part I: Sailor Shanties (London: J. Curwen & Sons, 1921), No. 9, \"Shenandoah\" (Windlass and Capstan), pp. 20 and 21, the voice part, as sung to Terry by Capt. Robertson; printed in E-flat, \"Slowly and with much expression\", in bars of 4/4 and 3/4, and written here in F: the print's pitches and syllables note for note, its free rhythm evened into 4/4 (held notes on \"you\", \"wide\" and \"sou-\" longer, the dotted \"bound to\" and \"'Cross the\" even); read from the University of Toronto Faculty of Music Library's copy at archive.org/details/shantybooksailor00terr (scan pages n41 and n42)"
 },
